# Rule: SwiftData Storage Infrastructure

Sources

https://developer.apple.com/documentation/swiftdata/modelcontainer
https://developer.apple.com/documentation/swiftdata/modelcontext
https://developer.apple.com/documentation/swiftdata/defaultstore
https://developer.apple.com/documentation/swiftdata/datastore
https://developer.apple.com/documentation/swiftdata/datastorebatching
https://developer.apple.com/documentation/swiftdata/preserving-your-apps-model-data-across-launches

Scope

Applies to:

- `ModelContainer` lifecycle and dependency injection
- `ModelContext` ownership boundaries
- `ModelConfiguration` store setup (in-memory, read-only, app group, CloudKit)
- custom `DataStore` adoption and batching integration

Intent

Establish explicit storage boundaries so persistence behavior is deterministic
across app runtime, tests, and production sync environments.

Core Principle

`ModelContainer` defines schema/storage boundaries; `ModelContext` owns model lifecycle operations.

Implementation Rules

- Create and inject a single authoritative `ModelContainer` per app environment boundary.
- Use context operations (`insert`, `fetch`, `delete`, `save`) through the owning context.
- Do not access persistence operations directly from arbitrary UI leaf nodes.
- Use `ModelConfiguration` for explicit storage behavior (in-memory, read-only, app group, CloudKit database).
- Keep test storage isolated with in-memory containers.
- For custom store implementations, conform to `DataStore` (and `DataStoreBatching` where needed)
  only when default store behavior is insufficient.

Canonical Patterns

```swift
WindowGroup {
    RootView()
}
.modelContainer(for: [Invoice.self, Client.self])
```

```swift
@Environment(\.modelContext) private var modelContext
modelContext.insert(newInvoice)
try modelContext.save()
```

Anti-Patterns

- Multiple ad hoc containers created per feature without explicit boundary reasons.
- UI components creating independent contexts casually.
- Production/test stores sharing configuration.
- Ignoring container configuration needs in CloudKit or app-group scenarios.

Checklist

- Is container setup centralized and explicit?
- Are context operations scoped to appropriate layers?
- Are model configurations correct for runtime environment (prod/test)?
- Is custom store usage justified and bounded?

Architectural Implications

- Container/context boundaries are architectural seams for data ownership and mutation flow.
- Store configuration choices influence durability, sync behavior, and test determinism.
- Custom datastore adoption increases complexity and must be justified by clear capability gaps.

Advanced Notes

- Environment-provided `modelContext` depends on an attached model container; missing container leads to unusable schema-less context behavior.
- Context save/fetch lifecycles should be observable and bounded for debugging and reliability.
- In-memory test containers prevent cross-test pollution and accidental production-store coupling.

Integration With Architecture

- Keep container bootstrap centralized in app composition layers.
- Keep services, actors, and explicit scene contexts as primary persistence interaction boundaries.
- Ensure test targets create dedicated in-memory containers per test lifecycle.

Enforcement Summary

Always prefer:

- centralized container construction
- explicit context ownership and operation boundaries
- environment-appropriate model configuration
- isolated in-memory test stores

Never allow:

- ad hoc container proliferation
- leaf-view persistence infrastructure ownership
- shared prod/test store setup
- custom datastore complexity without hard requirements
